But couldn't we then say the same for `@Column({ generated: true, unique: true, primary: true})` and every combination of `@Generated()`  `@Unique()` `@PrimaryColumn()` `@PrimaryGeneratedColumn()`? Or `@Index({unique: true})` and `@Unique()`, which actually are different, but only in certain drivers? In both cases it's a choice between being explicit or concise, or even ultimately a matter of style. Personally I prefer to use `@Unique()`/`@Index()` only for multi-column indices.

If you really want to solve the user confusion with these sort of things I think we need to move towards separate documentation and auto generated API reference. The user should be able to look up `ColumnOptions` and see that `generated: true` is the same as `Generated()` and vice-versa, rather than someone having to manually document the little details (of which there are many) in `docs/`. It also wouldn't be all that difficult to have all the `MetadataArgsStorage` additions check if there are duplicate definitions and throw a warning or error.

----

What I've tried to do here https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/pull/7058/commits/b032f6b0ef9be87fbd4ad7813a382519f9ada11a https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/pull/7058/commits/63c9bd95eb82e5c55e73c06b392e0c520a519c1d https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/pull/7058/commits/b321c020be76f1a90647728e5c939830a66474ef is at least have all the duplicate functionality use the same code by calling decorators within decorators. As long as this is the case I don't see any harm in having both `@Column({option: ...})` and `@Option`.

----

Also slight correction just in case you misread rather than made a typo, it would be:

```ts
@ManyToOne(() => Type, type => type.property, {
   index: {option1: ... }
})
```

Rather than:

```ts
@ManyToOne(() => Type, type => type.property, {
   option1: ... 
})
```

Which is a bit more obvious as to what is happening.

@pleerock 
The rationale is that indexing a FK is a common enough idiom (and most likely a best practice) that it should be encouraged by the API design as well something that should be very ergonomic to do. But as the linked issue mentions, this is mostly a quality-of-life improvement, it is not by any chance something that was not possible before.

Although, I do agree that "many ways of doing the same thing" is a problem and one that TypeORM definitely suffers from (sometimes with subtle differences between them) and it can confuse users. I myself haven't stopped to consider what happens in a case like
```typescript
@Index({/* indexOptions */})
@ManyToOne(() => Type, type => type.property, {
  index: { /* indexOptions */  }
})
```
